  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through next Friday)\nIssued at 111 PM CDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nInto Mon-Tues, the upper ridge is expected to be positioned over\nthe SE CONUS, while an upper low currently off the coast of the \nPacific NW tracks onto the northern Great Plains. While this high \nwill be centered to our east, it will also intensify, such that \nmid-level height values don't change appreciable. As such, the \nwell above normal temps persist, with guidance depicting a very \ngradual cooling trend as temps fall to the upper 80s/low 90s on \nthe Caprock by Thurs, with mid/upper 90s off the Caprock. \n\nAnother cold front associated with that northern Plains low will\ndrop south towards the TX Panhandle on Tues, but appears likely to\nstall north of our area. SW flow between troughing out west and \nridging to the east will place the monsoonal moisture plume near \nour area, but likely focused a bit to the north/west. Still, this \npattern should support occasional storm chances during the mid-\nweek period. PoPs increase with northwest extent (closer to the \nmonsoonal moisture plume and the occasional mid-level \ndisturbances), such that the NW portions of the FA have several \ndays with 40-50% (or higher) rain chances, while SE portions of \nthe FA may see little to no rain through Thurs.\n\nLate in the period, another upper wave digs from the Pacific NW\nand is progged to eject further south than prior waves, reaching \nthe central Plains Friday. This more southerly track favors the \nassociated cold front finally pushing across our FA, which would \nprovide some relief from the relentless summer heat. The latest \ndeterministic NBM drops highs into the 80s across most of the FA \nby Sat. It's a welcome sight, but as we've seen in previous weeks \nthis upper ridge can be quite stubborn to break down, and the \nensemble spread still encompasses a wide range of outcomes, \nranging from low 80s to upper 90s depending on the actual \nevolution of this late week cold front.",
